    <back>
      <floats-group id="article-18672.s16">
        <fig id="article-18672.image.f1" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Brugada Syndrome Type 1 Morphology. Electrocardiogram leads V5 and V6 are placed&#x000a0;1 intercostal space higher than usual: V5 at the 4th intercostal space,&#x000a0;1 space above the standard position of V1 (normally at the 5th intercostal space), and V6 at the 3rd intercostal space,&#x000a0;1 space above the usual V2 placement. Type 1 morphology is observed in leads V1, V1 at the 3rd intercostal space, and V2 at the 3rd intercostal space. Additional findings include a horizontal QRS axis, broad P waves, wide S waves in lateral and inferior leads, and QRS fractionation in leads III and aVL. Contributed by P.G. Postema, M.D., AMC</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="1280px-Brugada_syndrome_type1_example1_xCardioNetworks_ECGpediax" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-18672.s17">
        <fig id="article-18672.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Brugada Syndrome Electrocardiographic Patterns. Comparison of normal electrocardiogram leads V1, V2, and V3 (left) with Brugada syndrome types 1, 2, and 3 morphologies (right). Type 1 shows a coved ST elevation with a downward T wave. Type 2 displays a saddleback ST elevation.&#x000a0;Type 3 exhibits either coved or saddleback ST elevation of lesser magnitude. These distinctive patterns are crucial for diagnosing Brugada syndrome, associated with the risk of ventricular arrhythmias and sudden cardiac death. Image courtesy S Bhimji MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="brugada" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
